# The repository-wide .gitignore is written for .NET and Node build output, and it
# excludes three words this exploration uses as SOURCE directory names: `[Ll]ogs/`,
# any directory called `gen`, and `[Bb]in/`. Without these re-inclusions a slice
# agent commits its generator, its measurement binaries and its raw logs as nothing
# at all, and finds out a session later. That has now happened three times, once per
# word, so the rule is: a new source directory here is checked with `git check-ignore`
# before it is filled, not after.
#
# Four times now, not three: `poc/*/gen/` does not match a generator one level
# deeper, and `poc/python/mech/gen/` was swallowed whole. The re-inclusions below
# are therefore depth-independent. A pattern that covers the layout someone
# already wrote is not the same as one that covers the layout the next slice will
# write.
#
# `schema/` sidesteps the first two by avoiding the names. The slices cannot:
# `poc/<lang>/gen/` and `logs/<lang>/` are what README section 12 tells them to
# write, and `src/bin/` is where a Cargo harness keeps its binaries.
!logs/
!logs/**
!poc/**/gen/
!poc/**/gen/**

# ...but a `gen` inside a BUILD tree is output, not source, so re-exclude those.
# Making the re-inclusion depth-independent immediately un-ignored two CMake
# build directories' generated protobuf sources, which is the same defect in the
# other direction: a pattern wide enough to catch every source directory is wide
# enough to catch output too. Later rules win, so these come after.
poc/**/build/
poc/**/build-*/
poc/**/target/
poc/**/target-*/
!**/bin/
!**/bin/**
